1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,200
Saving a note is easy. Returning is harder. Recall Mansion turns notes into rooms. Ghosts show which cards are due.

2
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:19,920
I create a room for a short note about the Moon. Notes stay in this browser. No account or external AI service is required.

3
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:29,920
I choose reflects sunlight as the phrase to remember. The preview shows what will be hidden. My selection creates the question.

4
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:39,120
A new card is immediately due. I choose confidence before revealing the answer, keeping my expectation separate from the result.

5
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:50,520
After revealing, I rate my recall. Again returns in ten minutes. Hard returns in one day. Remembered advances through a simple schedule.

6
00:00:50,520 --> 00:01:00,520
Here I choose Again. The app saves one review and its real next due time, without pretending that ten minutes have passed.

7
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:11,680
With nothing currently due, the window lights up. Confident followed by Again gets a second look. This records my judgment, not a memory score.

8
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:24,040
I export a JSON backup. Import checks it before I explicitly choose to replace saved data. Otherwise, clearing browser storage removes local notes.

9
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:35,920
Kiro generated the requirements, design, and tasks. These are the actual files. The repository preserves the Kiro folder and an honest contribution record.

10
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:48,760
Kiro implemented the study core and tests. Codex built the interface and reviewed integration. All seventy three tests pass, including regressions from browser testing.

11
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:57,840
A small note, an honest check, and a reason to return. Recall Mansion gives a useful study routine a haunted home.
